// #2246 CHARACTERISATION, NOT A FIX. A green thread's stack is fixed at
// `schedStackBytes` and does not grow, and overflowing it is still an
// undiagnosed SIGSEGV (exit 139, no output on either stream). Nothing here
// changes that. What this program does is pin the two numbers that decide how
// deep a green thread may go, and prove end to end that the constant translates
// into stack a program can actually use — so a future change that quietly eats
// green-thread headroom reddens a diff instead of shipping.
//
// WHY THIS IS THE SIBLING ../deeprecursion NEVER HAD. That program recurses on
// `main`, which gets 8 MiB through the oversized-stack registry (#1843). Every
// other task in the process gets 256 KiB, which is 32x less, and no case
// exercised that budget at all. `main` is also the ONLY task whose size is a
// free parameter: 256 KiB is the masked-identity granule (`sched.bit`'s
// `schedStackBytes` — identity is `sp - sp % schedStackBytes` plus a tag at the
// base), so it is simultaneously the alignment unit and the largest a masked
// stack can be. It cannot be raised without replacing that mechanism.
//
// MEASURED, arm64-macos, self-hosted `bit` 0.1.0-dev and brew 0.1.8 alike, with
// a 48-byte frame, bisected against the PRE-#2613 64 KiB granule (not
// re-bisected since; the mechanism, not the exact byte count, is what this
// pins), deterministic to one frame in both directions:
//
//   inside spawn   1362 calls return, 1363 crashes  ->  65424 bytes = 64 KiB - 112
//   on main      174759 calls return, 174760 crashes -> 8388480 bytes = 8 MiB - 128
//
// Both budgets land within one frame of their declared constant, which is what
// identifies the fault as the first access at or below `stackBase` — the
// unmapped hole `mapGuardedStack` leaves there. The OS crash report agrees:
// `KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS at stackBase - 32`, inside a GAP of 0x8000 bytes.
//
// THE DEPTH IS DELIBERATELY FAR FROM THE CLIFF. 600 frames is 28800 bytes on
// arm64, 11% of the (post-#2613) budget, and x86-64 frames for the same
// recursion run bigger (../deeprecursion measured that difference the hard
// way, on real hardware). A case sitting at 90% of a per-arch budget is a
// flake, not a gate; the exact numbers are pinned by the two printed
// constants instead, which move on any edit to either, on every host, for
// free.

import { schedStackBytes, chanScratchWords } from "../../../runtime/sched"

// 600 * 48 = 28800 bytes of a 262144-byte budget on arm64. See the header.
const DEPTH: int = 600

// The ticket's own shape: one argument, no locals, so the frame is as small as
// this compiler emits and the depth is a byte budget divided by a constant.
fn down(n: int): int {
  if (n == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return 1 + down(n - 1)
}

// The recursion runs INSIDE the green thread, which is the whole point — the
// identical call on `main` has 32x the room and proves nothing about this.
fn worker(depth: int, done: chan<int>) {
  done <- down(depth)
}

fn main() {
  let c = chan<int>(1)
  spawn worker(DEPTH, c)

  // A returned count, not a bare "did not crash": an overflow that corrupts the
  // stack and survives far enough to print is caught as a wrong answer.
  println("green reached ${<- c}")

  // The budget itself, so it cannot move in silence. `granule` is the whole
  // stack; `scratch` is the per-task chan/select/error area that sits at the
  // LOWEST words of it (`scratch.bit`), i.e. inside the descent path rather
  // than below the guard — so it is subtracted from what a call chain may use.
  println("granule ${schedStackBytes}")
  println("scratch ${chanScratchWords}")
  println("usable ${schedStackBytes - chanScratchWords * 8}")
}
